# reading 2 of berkeley-principles-1.md by claude-opus-5-5
# Ad hominem: the modern philosophers' own principle (variation in appearance shows a quality is not in Matter) is turned against them.
# The principle is attributed to "modern philosophers" in S1-S2 and S4; Berkeley argues from it, so it also appears as a plain premise.
atom	varies(x)	x appears various to different perceivers or at different stations while the object remains unaltered
atom	inmatter(x)	x exists in Matter, without the mind, as a pattern of a real being
atom	sensible(x)	x is a sensible quality
domain	heat sweetness figure extension motion
P1	S2	∀x (varies(x) → ¬inmatter(x))	says philosophers
P2	S2	varies(heat) ∧ ¬inmatter(heat)	says philosophers
P3	S4	varies(sweetness) ∧ ¬inmatter(sweetness)	says philosophers
P4	S2	∀x (varies(x) → ¬inmatter(x))
P5	S3	varies(figure) ∧ varies(extension)	kind empirical
C1	S3	¬inmatter(figure) ∧ ¬inmatter(extension)	from P4 P5
P6	S5	varies(motion)	kind empirical
C2	S5	¬inmatter(motion)	from P4 P6
# The general conclusion rests on the unstated premise that every sensible quality varies in this way.
A1	-	∀x (sensible(x) → varies(x))
C	S1	∀x (sensible(x) → ¬inmatter(x))	from P4 A1
